1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,400
Multiply three milliseconds by a few
million gate-status polls per day and you
are no longer discussing elegance. You are
discussing a latency tax.

2
00:00:08,519 --> 00:00:15,919
Someone aimed a wide pointcut at every
public method under com.harbor..*, each
@Around allocating strings and writing
synchronous audit.

3
00:00:16,039 --> 00:00:26,640
On a cold path that already waits forty
milliseconds on the database, a few
microseconds of AOP vanish. On a hot
status loop, the abstraction becomes
measurable pain.

4
00:00:26,759 --> 00:00:41,280
AOP performance is about knowing where
that tax is worth paying. Startup cost
comes first. Auto-proxy creators scan
advisors, build candidate sets, and
generate JDK or CGLIB proxy classes for
matching beans.

5
00:00:41,399 --> 00:00:51,280
Hundreds of beans times class-based proxy
generation shows up in boot time and
metaspace. Narrow pointcuts and fewer
blanket aspects reduce how many beans get
wrapped.

6
00:00:51,399 --> 00:01:07,479
If only ten gate operations need auditing,
@annotation(Audited) beats execution(*
com.harbor..*(..)) across the tree. Look
at this java for a moment. Look at this
java for a moment. Runtime cost sits in
the call path.

7
00:01:07,599 --> 00:01:18,560
Prefer cheap advice bodies. An @Around
that serializes arguments to JSON and
writes synchronous network audit on every
call can dominate a method that used to be
a few field updates.

8
00:01:18,679 --> 00:01:26,519
Sample, bound, or async the observability
side effects. Avoid advising trivial
getters called thousands of times per
request.

9
00:01:26,639 --> 00:01:38,559
Remember self-invocation: moving logic to
public advised methods can add proxy hops
— or removing an annotation can change
nothing because calls never hit the proxy.
Pointcut shape matters.

10
00:01:38,679 --> 00:01:48,719
Complex runtime matching is cheaper when
Spring can cache and when static structure
dominates. Extremely wide expressions
force more proxying and more interceptor
evaluations.

11
00:01:48,839 --> 00:01:58,079
Named composed pointcuts that fail fast —
type first, then annotation — keep intent
clear and matching bounded. Measure before
you blame AOP.

12
00:01:58,199 --> 00:02:08,439
Profile or time representative gate
endpoints with aspects enabled and
disabled. Check allocation rates, not only
average latency. Watch for CGLIB proxy
class explosion in metaspace.

13
00:02:08,559 --> 00:02:15,479
Distinguish framework advisors you want —
transactions, security — from accidental
custom aspects on hot loops.

14
00:02:15,599 --> 00:02:26,959
A misconception is “remove all AOP for
speed.” You would reintroduce copy-pasted
logging and transaction demarkation,
usually with worse bugs and similar
overhead in handwritten form.

15
00:02:27,079 --> 00:02:39,159
Another is enabling exposeProxy = true and
calling AopContext.currentProxy()
everywhere to “fix” self-invocation —
sometimes correct, often a design smell
with thread-local coupling.

16
00:02:39,279 --> 00:02:49,759
Prefer restructuring so external calls
enter through the proxy naturally. We have
walked AOP from concepts through proxies,
advice, pointcuts, ordering, and cost.

17
00:02:49,879 --> 00:02:52,839
Cross-cutting concerns are under control
in the service layer.

18
00:02:52,959 --> 00:03:04,799
The next pressure is different: who may
call those services at all, how
credentials travel with a request, and
what “authenticated” means for an open
harbor API. That opens security
fundamentals.
